  "question_origin": "researcher_constructed",
  "question_text": "In Vespara a one-year fixed nominal-interest-rate loan is issued at 8%. At issuance, expected inflation for the year is 3%. Actual inflation is 7%. Use the approximate Fisher relationship: real interest rate equals nominal interest rate minus inflation. The consumer price index is 200 at the beginning of the year and 214 at the end.\n\n(a) Calculate the expected real interest rate at issuance. Show your work.\n\n(b) Calculate the realized real interest rate. Show your work.\n\n(c) Relative to expectations at issuance, who benefits from the unexpected inflation, the borrower or lender? Explain.\n\n(d) Calculate inflation from the consumer price index. Show your work.\n\n(e) If a worker’s nominal wage is indexed to rise by exactly 7% over this year, what happens to the real wage measured using this index? Explain.",
  "sources": [],
