      },
      "answer": {
        "answer_markdown": "**(a) Expected real interest rate at issuance**\n\nUsing the approximate Fisher relationship:\n\n\\[\nr_{expected}=i-\\pi_{expected}=8\\%-3\\%=\\boxed{5\\%}\n\\]\n\n**(b) Realized real interest rate**\n\n\\[\nr_{realized}=i-\\pi_{actual}=8\\%-7\\%=\\boxed{1\\%}\n\\]\n\n**(c) Effect of unexpected inflation**\n\nThe **borrower benefits** relative to expectations. Inflation was 7% rather than the expected 3%, so the loan is repaid in dollars with less purchasing power than anticipated. The lender receives a lower realized real return (1% rather than the expected 5%).\n\n**(d) Inflation from the CPI**\n\n\\[\n\\text{Inflation rate}=\\frac{CPI_{end}-CPI_{begin}}{CPI_{begin}}\\times100\n=\\frac{214-200}{200}\\times100=\\boxed{7\\%}\n\\]\n\n**(e) Real wage**\n\nThe real wage is **unchanged** using this index. The nominal wage rises by 7%, exactly matching the 7% increase in the CPI, so its purchasing power does not change.",
